course: adult-life
lesson: 5
title: Where money comes from
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Most of a family's money comes from pay for work, either wages by the hour or a salary for the year, arriving on payday.
explain: Some comes from elsewhere: the government helps with benefits such as Universal Credit and child benefit, and sometimes money is left in a will or a bonus arrives.
explain: Before pay reaches the bank, some is taken off as tax for the government, and some is put away into a pension for old age.
explain: Picture a terraced house in Manchester on the last Friday of the month: Mum checks her phone, smiles "It's payday, let's get a takeaway", and the kids cheer, because all week the answer to treats was "wait till payday".
explain: Children get their first taste of money through pocket money and small paid jobs, which is how grown-ups teach that money runs out.
fact: Wages are usually paid by the hour or the week, so more hours means more pay; a salary is a fixed amount for the year, split into monthly payments.
fact: Payday is the day pay arrives in the bank, often once a month, so money is usually lowest just before it.
fact: Many people are paid early in December, before Christmas, so January feels very long with the money already spent.
fact: Pocket money is a small regular amount given to children to practise choosing, saving and making mistakes with money.
fact: Some families pay children for extra jobs, like washing the car, but tidying your own room is just part of living together.
fact: Benefits are money the government pays to people who need help, such as those out of work, ill, caring for someone or on low pay; Universal Credit is the main one in the UK.
fact: Benefits are paid for by everyone's taxes as a safety net, and many families who work get some help too.
fact: Child benefit is a small regular payment from the government to families with children, to help with things like shoes and food.
fact: An inheritance is money or belongings left by someone who has died, as written in their will.
fact: Income tax is money taken from earnings for the government; people who earn more pay more, but "half of it goes to the taxman" is usually an exaggeration.
fact: Taxes pay for schools, the NHS, roads, police and benefits.
fact: National Insurance is another amount taken from pay; paying it in during working life gives people the right to a state pension when they are old.
fact: A pension is money saved during working life, often with the employer adding some, to live on after retiring; the state also pays a pension to most older people.
fact: A bonus is extra money on top of normal pay, for doing well or because the company had a good year; it is not guaranteed.
exercise: "I'm on a salary now, not paid by the hour." What is a salary? | A fixed amount for the year, paid in monthly parts | Pay that goes up and up with every single hour worked | Money paid only when the work is finished | A bonus paid at Christmas
exercise: Someone on wages works more hours this week. What happens to their pay? | It goes up | It stays exactly the same | It goes down | It is paid next year
exercise: Mum smiles: "It's payday, let's get a takeaway." What is payday? | The day her pay arrives in the bank | The day she has to pay all the bills at once | A holiday when shops are free | The day she pays the takeaway shop
exercise: A treat has to wait till payday. Why? | Money is usually lowest just before payday | Shops only open on payday, and close all the other days | Treats are only allowed once a month | Mum forgot to get money from the bank
exercise: "January is the longest month of the year." Why do grown-ups say it? | Pay came early in December and the money is spent | January really has more days than any other month of the year | It snows so the days feel longer | Schools are closed all January
exercise: "Here's your pocket money, don't spend it all at once." Why do parents give pocket money? | To let children practise choosing and saving | Because the law says they must give it every week | To pay children for going to school | So children can pay the family's bills
exercise: "I'll give you two pounds if you wash the car." What is this like? | A tiny first job, money for extra effort | A loan you must pay back later with a bit extra | A tax on washing the car | Pocket money that comes every week
exercise: Which job is usually NOT paid, just part of living together? | Tidying your own room | Washing the car | Cleaning the windows | Mowing a neighbour's lawn
exercise: "We're on Universal Credit for now." What is Universal Credit? | A benefit from the government for people who need help | A loan from a bank that must be paid back with interest | A card for shopping all over the world | A bonus from Mum's job
exercise: Who pays for benefits? | Everyone, through taxes | Only rich people, through gifts | The banks, from their profits | Nobody, the money is printed for free
exercise: "The child benefit has come in." What is child benefit? | A small regular government payment to families with children | Money children earn from doing chores around the house | A prize for children who do well at school in their exams | Money paid by schools to parents
exercise: "Grandma left us a little something in her will." What does it mean? | She left the family money or belongings when she died | She gave them a present for their birthday last year | She lent them money that they must pay back to her later | She left a bag behind at their house
exercise: "Half of it goes to the taxman." What is income tax? | Money taken from earnings for the government | A fee paid to a man who comes to collect rubbish | A charge for using a taxi | Money paid to the boss for having a job
exercise: Is "half of it goes to the taxman" usually exactly true? | No, it is usually an exaggeration | Yes, everyone pays exactly half | Yes, but only in January | No, nobody pays any tax at all
exercise: "National Insurance has gone up again." What does National Insurance build up? | The right to a state pension and some benefits | Insurance for the family car in case of a crash | Savings for a summer holiday | Money to pay the next bonus
exercise: "I'm paying into my pension." What is a pension? | Money saved now to live on after retiring | A small hotel for holidays by the sea | A fine for paying a bill late | Pocket money for grown-ups
exercise: "I got a bonus this year!" What is a sensible way to use it? | On one-off things, because a bonus is not guaranteed | On the monthly bills, since it will come again every month | Give it all back to the boss | Hide it so the taxman never knows
test: Overheard: "Did 50 hours this week, still same money, cos I'm salaried." Why no extra pay? | A salary is fixed, extra hours may earn nothing extra | He is on wages, which never change however long he works | His boss forgot to count the hours | Salaried workers are paid by the hour
test: Your brother says: "Mum's mean, she won't buy pizza till Friday." Put him right. | Friday is payday; waiting is planning, not meanness | He is right, Mum just doesn't like pizza and never will | Pizza shops are only open on Fridays | Mum is saving all her money for herself
test: Dad, mid-January: "Skint till the 31st, it's beans on toast, kids." Why so skint? | Christmas spending and an early December payday | His wages were cut in January by his boss | The banks close in January | Beans are cheaper only in January
test: Your friend spent all her pocket money on day one and can't buy the comic. What is the lesson? | Money runs out, and buying one thing means not buying another | Pocket money should always be spent at once, on the first day | Comics are a waste of money | Her parents should give her more
test: Your cousin says: "People on benefits are just lazy." Put him right. | Benefits help people who are ill, caring or on low pay, and many work | He is right, benefits are only for lazy people who don't want to work | Benefits are only for people who are rich | Nobody in work ever gets any help
test: Mum: "Child benefit's in, that's school shoes sorted." What is child benefit for? | Helping with the costs of bringing up children | Paying children for doing their homework every night | Buying presents for the parents | Paying the school fees of private schools
test: After the funeral Dad says quietly: "Your gran left us a bit." You feel strange about it. What is true? | It is fine to feel grateful and sad at the same time | You should feel only happy about the money and nothing else | You should refuse the money | It means Gran is not really gone
test: Uncle, grumbling: "Taxman takes the lot, what do we get for it?" What do taxes pay for? | Schools, hospitals, roads, police and benefits | Only the taxman's own salary and his big house | Nothing, the money is thrown away | Holidays for people in government
test: Grandad: "NI went up, so my take-home's down a tenner." Why is his take-home lower? | More is taken off as National Insurance | His boss cut his salary | He spent ten pounds on lunch | The bank charged him a fee
test: Mum: "Work puts in the same as I put in, so I'm doing it." What is she doing? | Saving into a pension, with her employer adding some | Paying extra tax to her boss every month from her pay | Buying shares in a shop | Lending money to her company
test: Dad: "Bonus! But don't count on it next year, kids." Why not? | A bonus is not guaranteed | Bonuses are only paid once in a life | He has to pay it all back | The taxman takes all of it next year
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the distinction between a salary and wages? | A salary is a fixed annual sum paid in instalments; wages vary with the hours worked | A salary varies with the hours worked; wages are a fixed annual sum paid in instalments | A salary is paid by the government; wages are paid by the employer | Both are sums paid exclusively upon retirement
exam: Identify the principal source from which state benefits are funded. | General taxation contributed by the population at large | Voluntary donations from wealthy individuals and charitable bodies | The profits of commercial banks | The pocket money of children
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning National Insurance. | A deduction from pay which establishes entitlement to a state pension and certain benefits | It is a policy insuring the employee's motor vehicle | It is a sum paid by the state to all employees annually | It is a deduction from pay which is returned in full to every employee each December
exam: Given that an employee is awarded a bonus, ascertain the most prudent manner of its application. | Upon non-recurring expenditure, the bonus not being guaranteed in future years | Upon regular monthly bills, the bonus being payable in perpetuity in every future year | Upon the repayment of the bonus to the employer | Upon the concealment of the bonus from the tax authorities
